In X-bar theory, adjuncts (such as prepositional phrases and relative clauses) adjoin to an intermediate projection ($\bar{X}$ / X'). Because the noun *"envoy"* takes a PP complement (*"to the duke"*), the structure creates two distinct $\bar{N}$ nodes to which the relative clause CP can adjoin. 

This yields two possible parses:
1. **High Attachment:** The CP adjoins to the higher $\bar{N}$ projecting from *"envoy"*. In this reading, it is the **envoy** who offended the emperor.
2. **Low Attachment:** The CP adjoins to the lower $\bar{N}$ projecting from *"duke"*. In this reading, it is the **duke** who offended the emperor.

The relevant X-bar phrase structure rules generating this ambiguity are:
* $N' \rightarrow N' \text{ CP}$ (Relative Clause Adjunction)
* $N' \rightarrow N \text{ PP}$ (PP Complementation/Adjunction)
* $N' \rightarrow N$

