Yes, the sentence **"Lara Croft decided in May she would raid more tombs"** is syntactically ambiguous. Specifically, it exhibits **PP-attachment ambiguity**.

### Linguistic Analysis

The ambiguity arises from the structural position to which the Prepositional Phrase (PP) *in May* attaches. In X-bar theory, adjuncts adjoin to an X-bar (X') or maximal projection (XP) level. The two possible parses yield distinct semantic interpretations:

1.  **VP-Attachment (High Attachment):** The PP adjoins to the matrix Verb Phrase (V'). 
    *   *Interpretation:* The decision was made in May. (The raiding of the tombs could happen at any time).
    *   *Formal Bracketing:* [IP [DP Lara Croft] [I' [I ∅] [VP [V' [V' [V decided] [CP she would raid more tombs]] [PP in May]]]]]

2.  **IP-Attachment (Low Attachment):** The PP adjoins to the embedded Inflectional Phrase (IP) within the Complementizer Phrase (CP). 
    *   *Interpretation:* The raiding of the tombs would take place in May. (The decision could have been made at any time).
    *   *Formal Bracketing:* [IP [DP Lara Croft] [I' [I ∅] [VP [V decided] [CP [C' [C ∅] [IP [IP she would raid more tombs] [PP in May]]]]]]]

*(Note: In Parse 2, the PP could also arguably adjoin to the embedded V' or VP, but adjoining to the embedded IP is the standard syntactic analysis for temporal adjuncts modifying an entire clause/event).*

