### Syntactic Analysis
Under X-bar theory, this yes/no question involves two instances of syntactic movement:
1. **T-to-C Movement (Subject-Aux Inversion):** The auxiliary verb *can* originates in the T head and moves to the C head to satisfy the [+Q] (question) feature in C. This leaves a trace ($t_i$) in T.
2. **Subject Raising:** The subject *I* originates in the Specifier of VP (the VP-internal subject hypothesis) and raises to Spec,TP to satisfy the EPP/D-feature. This leaves a trace ($t_j$) in Spec,VP.

The adverb *actually* is an adjunct adjoined to V', and the direct object *this* is the complement of V. For visual clarity and standard practice, the internal structure of the pronouns (DPs) is left flat unless specifically analyzing pronominal structure.

