To accurately represent the sentence "Natasha was likely to win the election" in X-bar theory, we must account for the fact that "likely" is a raising adjective. The subject "Natasha" is not semantically an argument of "likely" (Natasha is not likely herself; rather, it is likely *that she will win*). Therefore, "Natasha" is base-generated as the external argument of the embedded verb "win" and undergoes successive cyclic syntactic movement. 

Assuming the VP-Internal Subject Hypothesis, "Natasha" first moves from the embedded Spec,VP to the embedded Spec,TP (to satisfy the embedded EPP/check case), and then moves to the matrix Spec,TP (to satisfy the matrix EPP/check case). Both landing sites contain an indexed trace ($t_i$).

